notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-25T06:23:33.064Z","harness:claude-code","Multi-agent concurrency assessment 2026-07-25, covering the branch-merge and fleet-coordination halves together because they are usually confused for one problem.\n\nMerge safety is structurally complete and shipped: field-aware item merge, history union and re-anchoring, JSON merge for config and schema, a committed merge fence installed by init, storage-integrity validation, strict-exit history verification, and transaction-scoped garbage collection. What is not yet proven is that it stays complete under arbitrary divergence, which is why continuous multi-branch merge conformance (pm-76dnfg) exists as a property suite with a zero-conflict acceptance bar rather than as more point fixes. The open architectural question - whether field-level three-way merge is sufficient at thousands of agents or whether conflict-free replicated state is required - is deliberately held as a decision (pm-2sbj8p) blocked on that property suite plus the fault simulator, because it should be answered by measured conflict rates rather than by preference.\n\nCoordination is where the real gap was. Ownership safety is solved: claim is an atomic test-and-set, id allocation and stale-lock takeover races are closed, and cross-branch id collisions are addressed by configurable entropy plus duplicate detection. But safety is not efficiency. Thousands of agents ranking the same queue independently converge on the same head, waste claim attempts, and leave the critical path unstaffed - none of which any existing gate or benchmark measures. Fleet allocation (pm-3oqjnc) is filed to own that, with claim remaining authoritative so advice can never corrupt ownership, and with the explicit requirement that a fleet of one reproduces today's ranking exactly.\n\nThree open races remain tracked (config singleton read-modify-write, id allocation TOCTOU residual, stale-lock takeover residual) and they are deliberately the acceptance targets of the deterministic fault simulator rather than being fixed blind. That ordering matters: a race fixed without a reproducer is a race that returns.\n\nThe measurement gap worth naming: every concurrency claim in this epic is currently argued from code review rather than from a contention benchmark. Claim and lock throughput at scale (pm-lvyd) is the missing instrument, and it blocks the allocation work for exactly that reason - allocation cannot be shown to improve anything until collision waste is measurable."
body: "## Charter\nCapability epic for concurrency & multi-agent safety, created from the 2026-07-03 concurrency review. pm is agent-first: many agents mutate one git-backed workspace in parallel, so locking, claim semantics, id allocation, and singleton-file writes must be safe by construction. Review verdict: data integrity held under all parallel stress (no corruption, no history drift) — the five children are availability/atomicity gaps, not corruption bugs.\n\n## Delivery order (dependency-informed)\n1. pm-2muu lock-wait retry (in progress, implemented uncommitted) — foundation: gives every mutation a bounded wait.\n2. pm-8t5x claim test-and-set (in progress, implemented uncommitted) — relies on the item lock; with 2muu it is a true atomic test-and-set.\n3. pm-zwib stale-lock takeover race — related to 2muu (its removal cap bounds but does not fix the race).\n4. pm-khdq create/copy ID allocation TOCTOU (silent item overwrite — worst blast radius).\n5. pm-bgcu singleton-file (settings/schema/session/checkpoints) lock-free read-modify-write.\n\n## Verification bar\nEvery child closes with parallel-stress evidence recorded via pm test / notes (the review's empirical repros re-run green): N-parallel mutations self-heal, claims are exclusive, no lost config writes, no id collisions.\n\n## Boundary note 2026-07-06\nScale-out initiative pm-9rxu (under pm-o2kc) owns claim/lock THROUGHPUT at thousands of agents (child pm-lvyd). This epic keeps correctness races at any N (pm-zwib, pm-khdq, pm-bgcu) — they are prerequisites for meaningful throughput numbers."
